                <pb facs="0680" n="584" sameAs="frus:frus1872p1/pg584"/>

                <head>No. 433.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Sickles to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">United
                                States Legation in Spain</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Madrid</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1872-11-12"><hi rendition="#italic">November</hi> 12,
                        1872</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 471.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to forward herewith some
                    interesting information, respecting slavery in Cuba and Porto Rico, recently
                    communicated to me by the colonial minister. I also inclose a copy of a private
                    note to Mr. Gasset, pointing out the importance of obtaining periodical <pb
                        facs="0681" n="585" xml:id="pg585"/> returns from Cuba as a means of
                    ascertaining how far the emancipation act of 1870 answers its avowed
                    purpose.</p>
                <p>I transmit also a copy of the Gazette containing an official expression of the
                    thanks of the King in recognition of the voluntary manumission of a number of
                    slaves in Porto Rico. It is due to the present cabinet to state that it has
                    inaugurated this method of giving public sanction and praise to such acts, which
                    have before found no favor in the eyes of this government.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>D. E. SICKLES.</signed>
                </closer>












